00:06:47.826 --> 00:06:49.760
I do just off the cuff.

00:06:49.760 --> 00:06:55.010
So I focus more on our developer
experience, so documentation,

00:06:55.010 --> 00:06:59.280
tutorials, sample code,
and things like that,

00:06:59.280 --> 00:07:02.150
and also just taking a
holistic view of the platform

00:07:02.150 --> 00:07:04.250
and making sure
that developers who

00:07:04.250 --> 00:07:07.370
identify as Python developers
feel at home on the Google

00:07:07.370 --> 00:07:07.991
platform.

00:07:07.991 --> 00:07:08.490
MARK: Cool.

00:07:08.490 --> 00:07:13.200
So I don't do very much
Python, if any at all.

00:07:13.200 --> 00:07:15.230
So why Python?

00:07:15.230 --> 00:07:16.550
What makes Python great?

00:07:16.550 --> 00:07:18.841
JOHN WAYNE PARROTT: So Python
is one of those languages

00:07:18.841 --> 00:07:22.220
that you can absolutely
start your programming

00:07:22.220 --> 00:07:23.510
career with Python.

00:07:23.510 --> 00:07:26.390
It's a great language
for newcomers,

00:07:26.390 --> 00:07:27.890
because it's very approachable.

00:07:27.890 --> 00:07:30.810
But at the same time, it's also
an insanely powerful language.

00:07:30.810 --> 00:07:32.220
It's general purpose.

00:07:32.220 --> 00:07:35.282
It's used almost everywhere.

00:07:35.282 --> 00:07:37.490
It's hard to find places
where you don't see somebody

00:07:37.490 --> 00:07:39.587
using Python.

00:07:39.587 --> 00:07:42.170
And it's an incredible language,
because it's so approachable,

00:07:42.170 --> 00:07:44.750
but at the same time, you
can do so much with it.

00:07:44.750 --> 00:07:46.700
And it can really
take you a long way.

00:07:46.700 --> 00:07:49.970
So for me personally, I think
Python is really appealing

00:07:49.970 --> 00:07:53.480
because I came from a C++
game development background.

00:07:53.480 --> 00:07:56.120
And programming was always
this thing that I imagine.

00:07:56.120 --> 00:07:58.230
It's always going
to be difficult.

00:07:58.230 --> 00:08:00.170
It's never going to be easy.

00:08:00.170 --> 00:08:03.110
And then when I discovered
Python, I'm like, wow,

00:08:03.110 --> 00:08:04.610
this can be easy.

00:08:04.610 --> 00:08:08.000
I can actually express programs
in a way that makes sense,

00:08:08.000 --> 00:08:10.220
and I don't have to
struggle with this all day

00:08:10.220 --> 00:08:12.620
long just to do
something simple.

00:08:12.620 --> 00:08:14.930
So I think there's a
lot of appeal in Python.

00:08:14.930 --> 00:08:22.670
And I think especially for web
applications and data analysis

00:08:22.670 --> 00:08:26.530
applications, it's really
approachable and easy to use.

00:08:26.530 --> 00:08:29.379
FRANCESC: So since we're
talking about the kind of things

00:08:29.379 --> 00:08:30.920
you can do with
Python, why don't you

00:08:30.920 --> 00:08:33.419
tell us a little bit about the
things you can do with Python

00:08:33.419 --> 00:08:34.760
on the Google Cloud platform?

00:08:34.760 --> 00:08:37.130
What is the involvement
of Google Cloud platform

00:08:37.130 --> 00:08:38.570
with Python?

00:08:38.570 --> 00:08:42.667
JOHN WAYNE PARROTT: So
there's essentially--

00:08:42.667 --> 00:08:44.750
I guess you can think of
it like it's two buckets.

00:08:44.750 --> 00:08:48.170
There's people who are writing
web applications in Python.

00:08:48.170 --> 00:08:49.970
And of course,
Google Cloud Platform

00:08:49.970 --> 00:08:51.940
has multiple hosting options.

00:08:51.940 --> 00:08:55.286
You have Container Engine,
Compute Engine, App Engine.

00:08:55.286 --> 00:08:56.660
And Python works
on all of those.

00:08:56.660 --> 00:08:59.030
So you can run your
Python web application

00:08:59.030 --> 00:09:03.124
on Google Cloud Platform,
and we'll host it for you.

00:09:03.124 --> 00:09:05.540
And then on the other side of
things, all of our services,

00:09:05.540 --> 00:09:10.520
such as Datastore storage
and, newly, Spanner,

00:09:10.520 --> 00:09:12.650
you can talk to
those using Python.

00:09:12.650 --> 00:09:16.100
So you can use our services with
our Python client libraries.

00:09:16.100 --> 00:09:18.740
And even more than that,
things like Dataflow.

00:09:18.740 --> 00:09:22.396
You can use Python to program
your Dataflow pipelines

00:09:22.396 --> 00:09:23.270
and things like that.

00:09:23.270 --> 00:09:25.304
So the Python is pretty
much-- everything

00:09:25.304 --> 00:09:26.720
that you can do
on Cloud Platform,

00:09:26.720 --> 00:09:27.678
you can do with Python.

00:09:27.678 --> 00:09:28.850
MARK: Cool.

00:09:28.850 --> 00:09:31.730
Now, the amount that
I know about Python--

00:09:31.730 --> 00:09:35.270
I know there was the
time the crystal cracked,

00:09:35.270 --> 00:09:36.680
the great schism.

00:09:36.680 --> 00:09:42.409
So what version of Python
do we generally support?

00:09:42.409 --> 00:09:44.700
I don't even know what the
ecosystem really looks like.

00:09:44.700 --> 00:09:45.650
What's the deal with--

00:09:45.650 --> 00:09:46.610
is it 2 versus 3?

00:09:46.610 --> 00:09:47.950
Is that what's going on there?

00:09:47.950 --> 00:09:49.324
JOHN WAYNE PARROTT:
So 2 versus 3

00:09:49.324 --> 00:09:51.320
has been the debate
for many years.

00:09:51.320 --> 00:09:55.052
So 3 came out several years ago.

00:09:55.052 --> 00:09:55.635
I say several.

00:09:55.635 --> 00:09:59.270
It was a long time ago
now when 3.0 came out.

00:09:59.270 --> 00:10:02.010
And there were a lot of--

00:10:02.010 --> 00:10:06.040
it was non-trivial to upgrade
a project from 2.7 to 3--

00:10:06.040 --> 00:10:08.230
well, 2.6 at the time to 3.

00:10:08.230 --> 00:10:10.060
So a lot of people
just stayed with 2.

00:10:10.060 --> 00:10:14.060
Because if it's not
broke, don't mess with it.

00:10:14.060 --> 00:10:17.050
So that caused a
little bit of trouble.

00:10:17.050 --> 00:10:20.570
And a lot of libraries,
you know such as Django,

00:10:20.570 --> 00:10:23.140
didn't port right away.

00:10:23.140 --> 00:10:25.764
And that made it a lot harder
for people to adopt Python.

00:10:25.764 --> 00:10:28.180
Because not only do they have
to change their application,

00:10:28.180 --> 00:10:31.706
but the libraries they depend
on don't support by Python 3.

00:10:31.706 --> 00:10:33.580
Now, the story is
completely different today.

00:10:33.580 --> 00:10:35.620
Almost every major
Python package

00:10:35.620 --> 00:10:39.130
supports both Python 2 and 3
with the exception of maybe one

00:10:39.130 --> 00:10:41.140
or two, and there's
replacements for those.

00:10:41.140 --> 00:10:44.020
And in general, the community
is very focused on Python 3

00:10:44.020 --> 00:10:44.886
is the future--

00:10:44.886 --> 00:10:45.760
well, not the future.

00:10:45.760 --> 00:10:46.840
It's the present.

00:10:46.840 --> 00:10:49.750
And we need to get
there as fast as we can.

00:10:49.750 --> 00:10:52.960
The other thing that's driving
this adoption of Python 3

00:10:52.960 --> 00:10:57.430
is that Python
Software Foundation

00:10:57.430 --> 00:11:00.970
is going to stop supporting
Python 2 in 2020.

00:11:00.970 --> 00:11:03.550
So they've been given a
doomsday, essentially.

00:11:03.550 --> 00:11:05.230
There's actually a
Python doomsday clock

00:11:05.230 --> 00:11:08.950
you can pull up that gives you
a countdown to 2020 when we can

00:11:08.950 --> 00:11:11.830
stop worrying about Python 2.

00:11:11.830 --> 00:11:13.260
So it's pretty
interesting stuff.

00:11:13.260 --> 00:11:17.530
So in terms of where we focus--

00:11:17.530 --> 00:11:19.780
we as in Google Cloud
Platform focus--

00:11:19.780 --> 00:11:23.615
we definitely want
to support Python 3

00:11:23.615 --> 00:11:25.240
while not leaving
behind the people who

00:11:25.240 --> 00:11:26.320
are still using Python 2.

00:11:26.320 --> 00:11:29.080
Because there is still a lot of
people who are using Python 2.

00:11:29.080 --> 00:11:30.788
So when you look at
our client libraries,

00:11:30.788 --> 00:11:33.490
and you look at Container Engine
and Compute Engine and App

00:11:33.490 --> 00:11:37.350
Engine flexible
environment, Python 3

00:11:37.350 --> 00:11:39.760
is available and supported.

00:11:39.760 --> 00:11:42.880
We support Python 3.5 right
now with our container runtime

00:11:42.880 --> 00:11:46.090
that runs on App Engine
Flexible and Container Engine.

00:11:46.090 --> 00:11:49.760
And we we're about to
add 3.6 very shortly.

00:11:49.760 --> 00:11:52.667
App Engine Standard
is a special case.

00:11:52.667 --> 00:11:55.000
It's still on 2.7, because
most of the applications that

00:11:55.000 --> 00:11:58.780
run on App Engine Standard
are pretty much using 2.7

00:11:58.780 --> 00:12:01.660
and have no need to update.

00:12:01.660 --> 00:12:03.530
But we are investing
in standard,

00:12:03.530 --> 00:12:06.530
so I would expect that
that might change soon.

00:12:06.530 --> 00:12:10.395
So keep your eyes open,
eyes peeled there.

00:12:10.395 --> 00:12:11.020
FRANCESC: Cool.

00:12:11.020 --> 00:12:15.220
So we can use App
Engine with Python 2.

00:12:15.220 --> 00:12:17.650
What is the frameworks
that people normally

00:12:17.650 --> 00:12:19.170
use for these kind of things?

00:12:19.170 --> 00:12:22.210
I know that Flask, I
think, is one of them,

00:12:22.210 --> 00:12:24.960
or it might be Puddle,
or maybe both, actually.

00:12:24.960 --> 00:12:27.070
I'm not very sure.

00:12:27.070 --> 00:12:29.020
Could you talk a little
bit about frameworks

00:12:29.020 --> 00:12:30.100
that we support?

00:12:30.100 --> 00:12:31.641
JOHN WAYNE PARROTT:
Sure, absolutely.

00:12:31.641 --> 00:12:34.660
So we don't really make the
framework choice for you

00:12:34.660 --> 00:12:36.640
in any of our hosting platforms.

00:12:36.640 --> 00:12:40.970
Of course, we try to
follow the community.

00:12:40.970 --> 00:12:42.742
And if you look at
the Python community,

00:12:42.742 --> 00:12:44.950
a lot of people, when they
think about Python in web,

00:12:44.950 --> 00:12:46.570
they immediately jump to Django.

00:12:46.570 --> 00:12:49.780
Django is one of the
largest open source projects

00:12:49.780 --> 00:12:53.110
within the Python community
and has a huge userbase.

00:12:53.110 --> 00:12:57.580
And there's lots of plug-ins
and things like that for Django

00:12:57.580 --> 00:13:01.480
that make it a lot easier
to accomplish tasks quickly

00:13:01.480 --> 00:13:02.710
with Django.

00:13:02.710 --> 00:13:06.010
So we definitely want to give
more attention to Django users,

00:13:06.010 --> 00:13:07.845
because it's a huge
community base.

00:13:07.845 --> 00:13:09.220
And it's actually
something we've

00:13:09.220 --> 00:13:11.303
been working on over the
last year, is making sure

00:13:11.303 --> 00:13:14.620
that there's tutorials that
fit in to a lot of what

00:13:14.620 --> 00:13:16.630
Django users are looking for.

00:13:16.630 --> 00:13:20.440
That said, the Python community
is actually about split,

00:13:20.440 --> 00:13:23.370
about an even split
between Django and Flask.

00:13:23.370 --> 00:13:26.950
Django is more this
larger framework.

00:13:26.950 --> 00:13:28.120
It's very opinionated.

00:13:28.120 --> 00:13:30.550
It tells you how to
structure your app,

00:13:30.550 --> 00:13:32.530
and it gives you
a lot of batteries

00:13:32.530 --> 00:13:34.750
included sort of thing.

00:13:34.750 --> 00:13:37.870
Flask is more like a spartan,
minimalist framework.

00:13:37.870 --> 00:13:40.150
I think it actually started
as an April Fool's joke,

00:13:40.150 --> 00:13:44.170
and people just liked
the idea, and it just

00:13:44.170 --> 00:13:45.160
kept going from there.

00:13:45.160 --> 00:13:48.370
So I personally am a Flask user.

00:13:48.370 --> 00:13:52.450
And all of our tutorials for
App Engine Flexible environment

00:13:52.450 --> 00:13:55.840
are written in Flask, because
it's just a lot easier

00:13:55.840 --> 00:13:57.900
to write a sample
application with Flask,

00:13:57.900 --> 00:14:00.358
because there's a lot less code
involved, a lot less files,

00:14:00.358 --> 00:14:02.650
and a lower barrier
of entry, essentially,

00:14:02.650 --> 00:14:05.320
than trying to have a Django
app for every tiny sample

00:14:05.320 --> 00:14:06.435
that we write.

00:14:06.435 --> 00:14:07.060
FRANCESC: Cool.

00:14:07.060 --> 00:14:11.050
So what about other domains
other than web servers?

00:14:11.050 --> 00:14:15.250
Is there any support
for NumPy related

00:14:15.250 --> 00:14:16.960
to Google Cloud Platform?

00:14:16.960 --> 00:14:18.935
I could imagine
something like that for--

00:14:18.935 --> 00:14:23.440
I don't know-- Dataflow
or Datalab or whatever.

00:14:23.440 --> 00:14:24.950
Do you know anything about this?

00:14:24.950 --> 00:14:26.991
JOHN WAYNE PARROTT: So
with Dataflow and Datalab,

00:14:26.991 --> 00:14:31.330
you have this access to this
other domain of data scientist

00:14:31.330 --> 00:14:31.900
stuff.

00:14:31.900 --> 00:14:33.970
And in general,
our platform will

00:14:33.970 --> 00:14:38.170
let you use whatever you want
and just get out of your way.

00:14:38.170 --> 00:14:41.140
So if you're using
our container runtime

00:14:41.140 --> 00:14:44.982
and running your data analysis
pipelines on Kubernetes,

00:14:44.982 --> 00:14:46.690
our container runtime
contains everything

00:14:46.690 --> 00:14:49.920
that you need to install
NumPy out of the box.

00:14:49.920 --> 00:14:52.690
That is to say, you just drop it
in your requirements.txt file,

00:14:52.690 --> 00:14:54.689
which is how you declare
dependencies in Python,

00:14:54.689 --> 00:14:55.600
by the way, guys.

00:14:55.600 --> 00:14:58.000
You just drop NumPy into
your requirements.txt,

00:14:58.000 --> 00:14:59.590
and then it works.

00:14:59.590 --> 00:15:01.360
It should just work.

00:15:01.360 --> 00:15:03.650
And so we really just--

00:15:03.650 --> 00:15:05.260
a lot of Python is--

00:15:05.260 --> 00:15:07.546
the community has a lot
of opinionated ideas

00:15:07.546 --> 00:15:08.920
about how to handle
dependencies,

00:15:08.920 --> 00:15:10.840
how to deal with
installing things.

00:15:10.840 --> 00:15:14.290
And we just want to support
that and get out of the way

00:15:14.290 --> 00:15:17.080
so that we don't block
that sort of thing.

00:15:17.080 --> 00:15:20.350
I think Datalab has NumPy
and SciPy pre-installed.

00:15:20.350 --> 00:15:23.140
So you don't even have to
install those on that one.

00:15:23.140 --> 00:15:25.810
I could be wrong on
that, so don't quote me.

00:15:25.810 --> 00:15:27.310
But if it's not,
I know it should

00:15:27.310 --> 00:15:29.980
be pretty straightforward
to install in Datalab.

00:15:29.980 --> 00:15:33.340
Dataflow, I think that's
special purpose-built

00:15:33.340 --> 00:15:35.630
for data processing.

00:15:35.630 --> 00:15:38.500
So I imagine you wouldn't
have any problem using

00:15:38.500 --> 00:15:44.344
your normal Python data
scientist-related libraries.

00:15:44.344 --> 00:15:45.010
MARK: Excellent.

00:15:45.010 --> 00:15:45.250
All right.

00:15:45.250 --> 00:15:45.970
Cool.

00:15:45.970 --> 00:15:49.090
So that sounds like we have at
least a pretty decent coverage

00:15:49.090 --> 00:15:50.800
across Python.

00:15:50.800 --> 00:15:53.161
But are there any new
releases or things

00:15:53.161 --> 00:15:55.660
that are being actively worked
on that Python developers can

00:15:55.660 --> 00:15:57.070
look forward to?

00:15:57.070 --> 00:15:58.111
JOHN WAYNE PARROTT: Yeah.

00:15:58.111 --> 00:15:59.360
I mentioned this.

00:15:59.360 --> 00:16:02.037
I hinted at this earlier.

00:16:02.037 --> 00:16:04.120
One of the things that
we're definitely doing is--

00:16:04.120 --> 00:16:05.500
at least in my team--

00:16:05.500 --> 00:16:07.270
is trying to make
sure there's plenty

00:16:07.270 --> 00:16:08.860
of tutorials for Django users.

00:16:08.860 --> 00:16:12.730
We definitely want Django users
to feel at home on Google Cloud

00:16:12.730 --> 00:16:13.481
Platform.

00:16:13.481 --> 00:16:14.980
And that means
highlighting products

00:16:14.980 --> 00:16:20.110
such as Cloud SQL, which is
our hosted MySQL solution.

00:16:20.110 --> 00:16:22.180
Because Django developers
are a lot more familiar

00:16:22.180 --> 00:16:23.830
with a relational
database management

00:16:23.830 --> 00:16:28.180
system than something like
Datastore, for instance.

00:16:28.180 --> 00:16:30.040
So we're focusing
a bit on Django

00:16:30.040 --> 00:16:32.110
over the coming months
and things like that.

00:16:32.110 --> 00:16:34.320
But there's also-- there's
always new products being

00:16:34.320 --> 00:16:35.860
released for Cloud Platform.

00:16:35.860 --> 00:16:38.620
Just the other day,
we released Spanner.

00:16:38.620 --> 00:16:41.860
And of course, that has a
Python client library on day 1,

00:16:41.860 --> 00:16:44.590
and you can use
Spanner from Python

00:16:44.590 --> 00:16:48.670
if you want an incredibly
high-scale database that

00:16:48.670 --> 00:16:50.950
can do SQL queries.

00:16:50.950 --> 00:16:52.030
MARK: That sounds cool.

00:16:52.030 --> 00:16:53.280
Now I'm actually just curious.

00:16:53.280 --> 00:16:55.260
We talked a couple of
times about Django.

00:16:55.260 --> 00:16:58.120
Are there particular
challenges around running it

00:16:58.120 --> 00:16:59.265
on Google Cloud?

00:16:59.265 --> 00:17:01.390
Are there particular
platforms that it's better on?

00:17:01.390 --> 00:17:03.040
Does it work better
on Kubernetes

00:17:03.040 --> 00:17:05.369
than it does on App Engine
or something like that?

00:17:05.369 --> 00:17:06.760
JOHN WAYNE PARROTT:
Yeah So I think it just

00:17:06.760 --> 00:17:07.801
depends on your workload.

00:17:07.801 --> 00:17:12.520
So Python-- I think if
you're doing some heavy data

00:17:12.520 --> 00:17:15.650
processing, clearly you
don't want to use App Engine,

00:17:15.650 --> 00:17:19.780
because App Engine is built
for stateless workloads.

00:17:19.780 --> 00:17:21.369
But if you're writing
web front ends,

00:17:21.369 --> 00:17:23.170
then App Engine
absolutely makes sense.

00:17:23.170 --> 00:17:25.660
So I think it just all
comes down to your use case.

00:17:25.660 --> 00:17:27.430
I think all of our
platforms are pretty

00:17:27.430 --> 00:17:29.410
well-suited for running Python.

00:17:29.410 --> 00:17:33.340
I think it's just a case
of what execution model is

00:17:33.340 --> 00:17:35.240
going to best match.

00:17:35.240 --> 00:17:38.050
MARK: We were just talking
specifically about Django.

00:17:38.050 --> 00:17:38.490
JOHN WAYNE PARROTT: Oh, Django.

00:17:38.490 --> 00:17:38.800
MARK: Yeah.

00:17:38.800 --> 00:17:40.690
Is there a particular one
that's better for that?

00:17:40.690 --> 00:17:43.023
JOHN WAYNE PARROTT: I would
say App Engine Flex is going

00:17:43.023 --> 00:17:46.390
to give you the absolute best
out-of-the-box experience with

00:17:46.390 --> 00:17:48.610
Django.

00:17:48.610 --> 00:17:51.550
We purpose-built the
developer experience

00:17:51.550 --> 00:17:56.500
for App Engine Flexible
to meet Python developers

00:17:56.500 --> 00:17:57.310
where they are.

00:17:57.310 --> 00:18:00.940
So for the most part, if
you have an application--

00:18:00.940 --> 00:18:03.920
a Python web application that
has a requirements.txt file,

00:18:03.920 --> 00:18:06.580
you can drop in a
really simple app.yaml

00:18:06.580 --> 00:18:10.330
and deploy it to App Engline
Flexible without any changes,

00:18:10.330 --> 00:18:11.279
typically.

00:18:11.279 --> 00:18:13.570
You may have to change the
port that it's listening on,

00:18:13.570 --> 00:18:14.450
but that is our goal.

00:18:14.450 --> 00:18:16.600
Our goal is to be able
to take any Django app

00:18:16.600 --> 00:18:18.212
and run it on App
Engine Flexible.

00:18:18.212 --> 00:18:19.670
FRANCESC: So I'm
assuming that what

00:18:19.670 --> 00:18:20.970
you were saying
about the port is

00:18:20.970 --> 00:18:23.261
that you will need to read
the port from an environment

00:18:23.261 --> 00:18:24.739
variable, something like that?

00:18:24.739 --> 00:18:25.780
JOHN WAYNE PARROTT: Yeah.

00:18:25.780 --> 00:18:27.710
We expose the port
environment variable,

00:18:27.710 --> 00:18:29.570
which is always 80 80.

00:18:29.570 --> 00:18:31.390
FRANCESC: That's [INAUDIBLE].

00:18:31.390 --> 00:18:33.971
For Go, we're basically working
on exactly the same thing.

00:18:33.971 --> 00:18:35.470
So you can just
drop it, and it just

00:18:35.470 --> 00:18:38.110
works, which is pretty nice.

00:18:38.110 --> 00:18:40.810
As a Go developer,
something that I'm slightly

00:18:40.810 --> 00:18:46.300
jealous about for App Engine
is your amazing NDB library.

00:18:46.300 --> 00:18:49.804
I don't know if you could
talk a little bit about it.

00:18:49.804 --> 00:18:51.470
JOHN WAYNE PARROTT:
Everybody loves NDB.

00:18:51.470 --> 00:18:54.670
Fun fact-- it was actually
written by Guido van Rossum.

00:18:54.670 --> 00:18:59.680
The inventor of Python
himself wrote the NDB library.

00:18:59.680 --> 00:19:02.880
So there's a lot of interesting
things in that library.

00:19:02.880 --> 00:19:06.700
Our App Engine Standard
users absolutely love it.

00:19:06.700 --> 00:19:08.470
However, it's not
currently available

00:19:08.470 --> 00:19:10.390
outside of App Engine Standard.

00:19:10.390 --> 00:19:13.390
But we know that users love
it, and we are definitely

00:19:13.390 --> 00:19:15.820
looking into bringing
in that library

00:19:15.820 --> 00:19:19.130
and making it available
to use everywhere.

00:19:19.130 --> 00:19:24.190
So we want to rebase it on
top of the cloud platform--

00:19:24.190 --> 00:19:28.154
Google Cloud Datastore API
instead of the App Engine

00:19:28.154 --> 00:19:30.070
Datastore API so that
you can use it wherever.

00:19:30.070 --> 00:19:33.820
FRANCESC: So for those that
do not know what NDB is--

00:19:33.820 --> 00:19:38.740
and actually, I don't even
know what NDB stands for.

00:19:38.740 --> 00:19:40.900
Could you use describe
it a little bit, please?

00:19:40.900 --> 00:19:41.941
JOHN WAYNE PARROTT: Sure.

00:19:41.941 --> 00:19:46.090
So NDB is our ORM, our Object
Relational Mapper for Datastore

00:19:46.090 --> 00:19:48.520
for App Engine Standard.

00:19:48.520 --> 00:19:52.360
It is named NDB because
it means New DB.

00:19:52.360 --> 00:19:54.600
Because there was a
previous library for App

00:19:54.600 --> 00:19:56.290
Engine Standard just called DB.

00:19:56.290 --> 00:19:59.460
FRANCESC: That's a slightly
disappointing name.

00:19:59.460 --> 00:20:01.960
JOHN WAYNE PARROTT: It's
a great name, I think.

00:20:01.960 --> 00:20:03.830
So yeah, it's just an ORM.

00:20:03.830 --> 00:20:06.620
So for Python developers,
if you're familiar with it,

00:20:06.620 --> 00:20:09.692
it's like SQL Alchemy
or Django's ORM.

00:20:09.692 --> 00:20:11.150
Then it's somewhat
similar to that.

00:20:11.150 --> 00:20:15.230
You define your data
models as classes,

00:20:15.230 --> 00:20:18.140
and then you can define
the property types on that.

00:20:18.140 --> 00:20:22.850
And then, of course, the library
handles querying, and then

00:20:22.850 --> 00:20:25.110
serialization, and
stuff like that.

00:20:25.110 --> 00:20:27.582
MARK: And it also puts a
Memcache in front of it, too?

00:20:27.582 --> 00:20:28.790
JOHN WAYNE PARROTT: Oh, yeah.

00:20:28.790 --> 00:20:31.910
That's one of the really
cool features about NDB.

00:20:31.910 --> 00:20:34.910
Because App Engine includes
a Memcache service,

00:20:34.910 --> 00:20:38.750
NDB does write-through caching
for all of your entities.

00:20:38.750 --> 00:20:40.570
So it's pretty neat.

00:20:40.570 --> 00:20:43.400
And it works about
95% of the time

00:20:43.400 --> 00:20:45.354
for everybody, which is great.

00:20:45.354 --> 00:20:47.270
That's one of the
difficulties with porting it

00:20:47.270 --> 00:20:48.320
outside of App Engine.

00:20:48.320 --> 00:20:50.540
Because you either
have to provide

00:20:50.540 --> 00:20:52.040
some sort of plug-in
interface to be

00:20:52.040 --> 00:20:54.350
able to add different caching
layers-- because imagine

00:20:54.350 --> 00:20:56.240
if you're running
on Compute Engine,

00:20:56.240 --> 00:20:58.220
you may not have Memcache
available to you.

00:20:58.220 --> 00:21:00.050
You may have Redis or
something like that.

00:21:00.050 --> 00:21:02.690
So you want to be able to plug
in a different caching back

00:21:02.690 --> 00:21:05.180
end, or not have a
caching back end if you

00:21:05.180 --> 00:21:06.540
don't have anything available.

00:21:09.380 --> 00:21:11.600
MARK: I'm curious--
is there anything

00:21:11.600 --> 00:21:13.430
new and exciting in
the Python community

00:21:13.430 --> 00:21:16.430
that you're particularly
excited about, new frameworks

00:21:16.430 --> 00:21:18.464
or releases or anything
interesting like that?

00:21:18.464 --> 00:21:19.630
JOHN WAYNE PARROTT: Oh, man.

00:21:19.630 --> 00:21:21.260
Where do I start?

00:21:21.260 --> 00:21:24.940
The Python community is always
growing and always evolving.

00:21:24.940 --> 00:21:29.150
With the release of 3.6, we
got some really neat stuff.

00:21:29.150 --> 00:21:31.160
We have this thing
called async I/O.

00:21:31.160 --> 00:21:33.620
And in 3.6, it's no
longer experimental.

00:21:33.620 --> 00:21:36.470
It's actually a
supported core part

00:21:36.470 --> 00:21:38.450
of the Python standard library.

00:21:38.450 --> 00:21:41.600
So async I/O gives us
something similar to Go's

00:21:41.600 --> 00:21:44.330
go routines in Python.

00:21:44.330 --> 00:21:47.600
So it allows us to
do this really cool

00:21:47.600 --> 00:21:49.940
co-routine asynchronous code.

00:21:49.940 --> 00:21:52.970
And people have done all
sorts of really cool things

00:21:52.970 --> 00:21:55.600
with this.

00:21:55.600 --> 00:21:57.071
I feel like maybe
late last year--

00:21:57.071 --> 00:21:58.820
I may be getting my
timelines wrong here--

00:21:58.820 --> 00:22:03.350
somebody released a plug-in
for Python's async I/O

00:22:03.350 --> 00:22:07.790
that uses UV loop underneath
the covers, which is, I think,

00:22:07.790 --> 00:22:11.720
the same invented I/O
that Node.js uses.

00:22:11.720 --> 00:22:13.860
So it's blazingly fast.

00:22:13.860 --> 00:22:15.630
So it's pretty cool.

00:22:15.630 --> 00:22:19.340
And then somebody else built a
web framework on top of UV Loop

00:22:19.340 --> 00:22:22.660
called Sanic, because
you have to go fast.

00:22:22.660 --> 00:22:27.710
So Sanic is one of the
fastest web frameworks ever.

00:22:27.710 --> 00:22:29.780
And it's just super cool stuff.

00:22:29.780 --> 00:22:34.100
So with async I/O, you can do
a lot more with one process,

00:22:34.100 --> 00:22:37.280
and you can do things like
use different event loops,

00:22:37.280 --> 00:22:41.870
like UV loop, and get insane
network performance out

00:22:41.870 --> 00:22:42.977
of your Python programs.

00:22:42.977 --> 00:22:44.810
So one of the things I
have on my list to do

00:22:44.810 --> 00:22:47.840
is actually write
an application using

00:22:47.840 --> 00:22:50.150
Sanic that runs on
App Engine Flexible

00:22:50.150 --> 00:22:54.350
so that we can be part of
this crazy, awesome movement

00:22:54.350 --> 00:22:56.770
of async stuff
going on in Python.

