00:27:06.510 --> 00:27:08.700
Francesc, you were
telling me earlier

00:27:08.700 --> 00:27:11.620
of a question that came from one
of the members of your workshop

00:27:11.620 --> 00:27:15.070
while you were having
your travels in India.

00:27:15.070 --> 00:27:18.070
He was talking
about transactions

00:27:18.070 --> 00:27:22.180
and being able to
transactionally store data,

00:27:22.180 --> 00:27:24.400
say, in some sort of
storage system, and then

00:27:24.400 --> 00:27:26.140
also being able to
send an email such

00:27:26.140 --> 00:27:28.240
that, say, the email failed.

00:27:28.240 --> 00:27:31.300
Then the transaction on the
data storage would draw back.

00:27:31.300 --> 00:27:33.850
Or if the data storage
didn't go through properly,

00:27:33.850 --> 00:27:35.470
then the email wouldn't go out.

00:27:35.470 --> 00:27:38.350
How can I do that
transactionally in that sort

00:27:38.350 --> 00:27:39.835
of situation on Google Cloud?

00:27:39.835 --> 00:27:40.460
FRANCESC: Yeah.

00:27:40.460 --> 00:27:44.590
So to be more specific, this was
in the context of App Engine.

00:27:44.590 --> 00:27:47.550
I have this Go Web
workshop that is basically

00:27:47.550 --> 00:27:50.260
writing Go web
applications on App Engine.

00:27:50.260 --> 00:27:52.310
And we're already
using Datastore.

00:27:52.310 --> 00:27:55.570
And I mentioned that you can
use the Mail API, Mail Service

00:27:55.570 --> 00:27:59.002
API from App Engine to
send emails very easily.

00:27:59.002 --> 00:28:00.460
And the question
was actually that.

00:28:00.460 --> 00:28:04.660
So imagine that I want to store
something in the data store

00:28:04.660 --> 00:28:07.120
after sending an
email, or that I

00:28:07.120 --> 00:28:10.431
want that email only to be sent
when the Datastore thing has

00:28:10.431 --> 00:28:10.930
worked.

00:28:10.930 --> 00:28:15.100
So I want to basically have
the sending of an email

00:28:15.100 --> 00:28:17.480
as part of a transaction.

00:28:17.480 --> 00:28:20.260
This is something that
you cannot really do.

00:28:20.260 --> 00:28:24.460
But there's actually ways of
mitigating that issue, which

00:28:24.460 --> 00:28:27.610
is one of the best ways
to do it is actually

00:28:27.610 --> 00:28:30.010
by using task queues.

00:28:30.010 --> 00:28:32.791
So task queues-- I think we've
talked about it a long time

00:28:32.791 --> 00:28:33.290
ago.

00:28:33.290 --> 00:28:35.050
But task queues
is a little thing

00:28:35.050 --> 00:28:36.820
that allows you to
schedule something

00:28:36.820 --> 00:28:39.320
to be done later
on at some point.

00:28:39.320 --> 00:28:41.290
So in this case,
you could say, oh, I

00:28:41.290 --> 00:28:43.810
want to store something
in the data store,

00:28:43.810 --> 00:28:45.785
and then I want to
schedule sending

00:28:45.785 --> 00:28:47.980
the email at some point.

00:28:47.980 --> 00:28:51.520
Now, the cool thing is
that storing something

00:28:51.520 --> 00:28:56.470
in the task queue can be done
inside of a transaction, inside

00:28:56.470 --> 00:28:58.030
of a Datastore transaction.

00:28:58.030 --> 00:29:00.520
So you can atomically
decide if you

00:29:00.520 --> 00:29:02.080
should be sending
that email or not

00:29:02.080 --> 00:29:06.850
according to the success status
of a transaction indexer, which

00:29:06.850 --> 00:29:08.320
is actually very powerful.

00:29:08.320 --> 00:29:10.510
Because then you can
use the same principle

00:29:10.510 --> 00:29:11.899
to apply it to
whatever you want.

00:29:11.899 --> 00:29:12.940
MARK: Oh, so that's cool.

00:29:12.940 --> 00:29:16.870
So if I am doing something in
Datastore with a transaction,

00:29:16.870 --> 00:29:19.360
I can then transactionally
also send off task queues

00:29:19.360 --> 00:29:20.860
to fire something
in the background.

00:29:20.860 --> 00:29:23.080
But if any part in
that chain fails,

00:29:23.080 --> 00:29:25.000
then those task queues
and that data storage

00:29:25.000 --> 00:29:26.680
will all roll back atomically.

00:29:26.680 --> 00:29:27.670
FRANCESC: Exactly.

00:29:27.670 --> 00:29:29.790
So you don't need
to go and be like,

00:29:29.790 --> 00:29:32.290
oh, I add something to the task
queue, and then it was like,

00:29:32.290 --> 00:29:33.650
oh, let me get it back.

00:29:33.650 --> 00:29:34.900
There's no need of doing that.

00:29:34.900 --> 00:29:36.460
You can just use a
transaction as you

00:29:36.460 --> 00:29:37.050
would do in a data store.

00:29:37.050 --> 00:29:37.617
So

00:29:37.617 --> 00:29:38.200
MARK: Awesome.

00:29:38.200 --> 00:29:39.940
So I could send an
email, or an SMS,

00:29:39.940 --> 00:29:41.890
or just do something
even totally crazy,

00:29:41.890 --> 00:29:43.990
like compute a whole new
thing if I wanted to.

00:29:43.990 --> 00:29:46.240
FRANCESC: Or you can lock some
metrics, or whatever cool thing

00:29:46.240 --> 00:29:46.865
you want to do.

00:29:46.865 --> 00:29:48.520
It is quite simple.

00:29:48.520 --> 00:29:49.309
MARK: Awesome.

